              "type": "reasoning.summary",
              "summary": "**Evaluating design constraints**\n\nI’m thinking about a multi-module layout centered around a forward docking node, especially in mature configurations. I wonder if saying “at least two directions” is too strict during partial phases. There are different varieties of configurations to consider, like a core-only variant or an early linear one with Kvant-1. I have to establish at least five constraints: configuration, docking seams, solar components, orbit, and exclusions for aircraft parts and truss from the ISS. I also question the relevance of a docking interface constraint.**Clarifying legal depiction constraints**\n\nI'm contemplating whether an illustration of deorbiting could legally depict Mir breaking up in the atmosphere. If all legal depictions are hard-defined, then outer space images wouldn’t be universal, though I wonder if I could show it during launch with a core module on the rocket. I think I should establish five constraints: architecture, docking, solar components, exclusions for aircraft, and ISS truss. Including ground negatives seems too broad, given museum mockups. I’d better avoid depicting the environment entirely for accurate representation.**Considering branch constraints**\n\nI’m thinking about the branch constraint related to the original single source. It might be possible to merge this into the configuration statement while only using sources like glm5.3. All architecture sources should include unique layout information. I want to clarify how constraints for variants look: the early stage would include just the core module, the transitional stage adds the core module plus docked segments, potentially in axial or branching configurations. Finally, the complete configuration would consist of the core plus five experiment modules that cluster and extend in multiple directions.",
